WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.560
Yeah, so the question was, um, there was a string here at the bottom that started with the character F.

00:10.560 --> 00:15.760
Are there any other types of special strings like that in Python?

00:15.760 --> 00:20.800
Yeah, you might see R which stands for a regular expression.

00:20.800 --> 00:26.400
You might see B which is a byte string, um, there are a few others

00:26.480 --> 00:32.000
that I, um, can't remember off the top of my head, but pretty easy to look up.

00:32.000 --> 00:36.880
Import requests. It says my, uh, the request module is missing.

00:36.880 --> 00:42.960
That's because the request library is not part of Python standard library, so everything that

00:42.960 --> 00:49.520
comes with the Python installation when you pull it down. Um, so in order to get this code to work,

00:49.520 --> 00:56.080
you're going to have to install the request library with a different tool that we'll talk about later.

00:57.040 --> 01:04.560
Oh, and I think I missed spoke earlier. If you see an R here, um, it's generally used for regular

01:04.560 --> 01:11.600
expressions, but it, uh, symbolizes a raw string. So when you're writing regular expressions,

01:11.600 --> 01:17.200
you might not want to do a lot of, uh, escaping like you would in a normal string. So it's just

01:17.200 --> 01:23.200
formatted a little bit differently. Not something you see much unless you are working with regular

01:23.200 --> 01:25.200
expressions.

